# --- SCOPE-DOCX: Exhibit A as an editable Word document (scope_docx.py) ---
# MIT, recorded in docs/ATTRIBUTIONS.md, approved by the user 2026-08-07. Pinned in the change that
# FIRST USES it, per the roadmap rule: a dependency carried ahead of its code is supply-chain surface
# for no benefit.
#
# Marginal cost is ONE package, not three: both of its requirements are already in the lock and both
# floors are already satisfied — lxml 6.1.1 (needs >=3.1.0) and typing-extensions 4.16.0 (needs
# >=4.9.0). Checked before adding rather than assumed.
#
# Why a library rather than hand-rolled OOXML: a .docx is a zip of XML and the exhibit is only
# headings and paragraphs, so writing it directly is tempting. It is refused because this is a
# CONTRACT document — a malformed part that Word declines to open is a far worse failure than a
# dependency, and the failure would land on a subcontractor rather than on us.
python-docx>=1.2

# --- security floors on TRANSITIVE packages -------------------------------------------------------
# Nothing here is imported by our code. Each line exists only to raise a floor on a package we get
# through something else, because a transitive dependency has no other place to be pinned. Delete a
# line once the parent's own floor has caught up — leaving it costs nothing but says something false
# about why the version is what it is.
#
# cryptography: arrives via pyhanko / pyhanko-certvalidator / signxml. CVE-2026-69247 (HIGH,
# GHSA-g6cj-pr64-35w5) is a Bleichenbacher oracle in PKCS#7 EnvelopedData DECRYPTION — distinguishable
# errors and timing. We only ever *sign* (PAdES via pyhanko, XML-DSig verification via signxml) and
# never decrypt EnvelopedData, so the exploit path is very likely absent from our surface. Floored
# anyway: "we don't call the vulnerable API" is an argument about today's code, and the fix is a
# version number. Found 2026-08-09 by auditing the LOCK — CI's pip-audit had been reporting it into a
# step summary that nothing failed on.
cryptography>=50.0.0
